---
/**
 * /plant-care/weather/ — how to switch on live weather.
 *
 * ── WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AT ALL ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The app cannot tell you where to get a weather key, and that is not an
 * oversight — it is the build guard working as designed. `scripts/emit.mjs`
 * fails the build if any weather provider's name or hostname appears anywhere
 * in the shipped file, because the brief was explicit that no provider may be
 * pre-wired or bundled: their free tiers are licensed for personal,
 * non-commercial use and this is a paid product.
 *
 * The consequence was a settings screen that said "supply your own address"
 * and then did not say where anybody might get one. Correct, and useless.
 *
 * So the instructions live here instead. A page on this site is not the
 * artefact: naming providers here is a recommendation to a reader, not a
 * service compiled into a product being sold. The app links to this URL —
 * harvestmath.com is the one hostname the guard permits in the build, because
 * the app already cites this site in its own copy.
 *
 * Keep this page accurate. It is the one piece of the product's documentation
 * that depends on somebody else's API, so it will drift, and it is the only
 * part that can be fixed without shipping a new build to anybody.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { LAUNCHED, PATHS, PRODUCT_NAME } from '../../data/plantCare';
---

<Layout
  title={`Live weather · ${PRODUCT_NAME}`}
  description="How to point HarvestMath Plant Care at a weather service of your own. Optional, free, and about five minutes."
  noindex={!LAUNCHED}
  canonical="https://harvestmath.com/plant-care/weather/"
>
  <section class="bg-sand-50">
    <div class="container-prose py-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-bark-500">
        <a href={PATHS.marketing} class="hover:text-moss-800">← {PRODUCT_NAME}</a>
      </p>

      <h1 class="mt-6 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-bark-900">Switching on live weather</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
        Optional, and off in every copy of the app as it ships. With it on, real rainfall pushes an outdoor plant's
        next watering back and a hot spell pulls every plant's forward. Five minutes to set up, and the app is
        completely usable without it — the zone and season data already covers most of the same ground.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-moss-100 bg-white p-5">
        <h2 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-bark-900">Why you have to supply the address</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
          Weather services that offer a free tier almost always license it for personal, non-commercial use. This app
          is a paid product, so bundling a call to one would be us using somebody's free tier commercially, on your
          behalf, without asking either of them or you. Instead you sign up as yourself, for your own use, and the app
          calls the address you give it — and nothing else, ever, and only when you press the button.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900">What the app needs</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
        Two numbers, from any service that will return JSON: <strong>a daily rainfall total in millimetres</strong> and
        <strong>a daily maximum temperature in Celsius</strong>. You paste the URL, and you tell the app where in that
        JSON the two numbers live. Nothing about the app is tied to a particular provider.
      </p>

      <!-- ── option one ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-moss-200 bg-white p-6">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-moss-700">The simplest one</p>
        <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl font-semibold text-bark-900">Open-Meteo — no account, no key</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
          Open-Meteo needs no sign-up and no API key at all for personal use, which makes it about a ninety-second job.
          Their free access is licensed for non-commercial use — if you are running a nursery or a business, read their
          terms and take their commercial licence rather than this route.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-medium text-bark-800">1. Paste this into the app, with your own coordinates:</p>
        <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-bark-900 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-sand-100"><code>https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.5&longitude=-0.13&daily=precipitation_sum,temperature_2m_max&timezone=auto&forecast_days=1</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-bark-500">
          Replace <code>51.5</code> and <code>-0.13</code> with your own. Right-click your house in Google Maps and the
          first line of the menu is your latitude and longitude; two decimal places is plenty.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-medium text-bark-800">2. The two field paths — these are the app's defaults, so
          there is nothing to change:</p>
        <div class="mt-2 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 p-3">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-bark-500">Rainfall</p>
            <code class="text-sm text-bark-800">daily.precipitation_sum.0</code>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 p-3">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-bark-500">Maximum temperature</p>
            <code class="text-sm text-bark-800">daily.temperature_2m_max.0</code>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-bark-600">
          3. Press <strong>Check the weather now</strong>. That is the whole thing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ── option two ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-moss-100 bg-white p-6">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-moss-700">If you would rather have an account</p>
        <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl font-semibold text-bark-900">WeatherAPI.com — free tier, with a key</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
          Sign up at weatherapi.com, take the free plan, and copy the key from your dashboard. It takes a postcode or a
          town name rather than coordinates, which some people find easier. Read their terms: the free plan is for
          personal use.
        </p>

        <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-bark-900 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-sand-100"><code>https://api.weatherapi.com/v1/forecast.json?key=YOUR_KEY_HERE&q=SW1A%201AA&days=1</code></pre>

        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 p-3">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-bark-500">Rainfall</p>
            <code class="break-all text-sm text-bark-800">forecast.forecastday.0.day.totalprecip_mm</code>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 p-3">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-bark-500">Maximum temperature</p>
            <code class="break-all text-sm text-bark-800">forecast.forecastday.0.day.maxtemp_c</code>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-bark-500">
          Paste those two paths into the "where the numbers are" fields, replacing the defaults.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ── anything else ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-moss-100 bg-white p-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-bark-900">Or any other service</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
          The app only wants two numbers out of a JSON response, so any provider works. To find the paths yourself,
          open your URL in a browser tab and read the JSON: the path is the names you would follow to reach the number,
          separated by dots, with <code>0</code> for "the first one in the list". So in
          <code>{'{"daily": {"rain": [4.2]}}'}</code> the path to 4.2 is <code>daily.rain.0</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ── when it does not work ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold text-bark-900">If it does not work</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-bark-900">
            "Reached the service, but could not find a number at …"
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            The URL is fine and the paths are wrong. Open the URL in a browser tab and check the names against what
            comes back — providers rename fields, and this page can be out of date while their JSON is not.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-bark-900">The request is refused, or nothing comes back</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            A page opened from your own disk is not allowed to call every server — some refuse requests that do not come
            from a website. Open-Meteo allows them, which is the other reason it is first on this page. If your provider
            does not, use the installable version of the app instead, or switch providers. Everything else in the app
            keeps working either way.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-bark-900">You are on a phone with no signal</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-600">
            Then there is no weather, and nothing else changes. The app reads the last figure it fetched for up to three
            days and then ignores it, because last Tuesday's rain is not today's.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-clay-200 bg-clay-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-bark-800">
          <strong>Your URL is stored inside your own plant file.</strong> If it contains a personal key, bear that in
          mind before sharing that file with anybody. Nothing is sent anywhere except to the address you typed, and only
          when you press the button — not on start-up, not in the background.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-bark-500">
        <a href={PATHS.marketing} class="hover:text-moss-800">← Back to {PRODUCT_NAME}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
